// KytyVulkan - Vulkan helper utilities for the Kyty PS5 emulator
// (Linux / Windows / macOS via MoltenVK).
//
// Features
//   1. Queue family selection: graphics plus dedicated compute / transfer families (async work).
//   2. ReBAR / SAM / UMA detection and a buffer allocator with fallbacks (direct CPU writes to
//      VRAM when it pays off, staging otherwise).
//   3. Sub-allocation: many small buffers share a few large VkDeviceMemory blocks, which keeps the
//      device far away from VkPhysicalDeviceLimits::maxMemoryAllocationCount.
//   4. Memory budget awareness (VK_EXT_memory_budget) to avoid paging and driver resets (TDR).
//   5. Timeline semaphores and a batched submit helper that never touches the heap.
//   6. On-disk pipeline cache with header validation and atomic writes.
//   7. MoltenVK support (portability enumeration / portability subset).
//   8. RAII wrappers, so early returns and exceptions cannot leak Vulkan objects.
//   9. Physical device selection: rejects GPUs without the required features, picks the best one.
//
// What it does NOT do: it does not bypass any protection mechanism and does not access hardware
// outside of the Vulkan API.
//
// Requirements: C++20, a Vulkan 1.2+ instance and device, and a linked Vulkan loader (or a
// function loader such as volk that provides the vk* symbols).
//
// Threading
//   * Allocator::CreateBuffer, Buffer::Reset, Allocator::Trim and Allocator::GetStats are
//     internally synchronized.
//   * Allocator::Flush / Allocator::Invalidate, SubmitTimeline and WaitTimeline take no locks.
//     Vulkan's external-synchronization rules for the objects you pass in still apply (for
//     example, a VkQueue must not be submitted to from two threads at once).
//   * Command pools are not thread-safe: use one pool per thread.
//
// Lifetime
//   * Every Buffer must be destroyed (or Reset) before the Allocator that created it, and the
//     Allocator must be destroyed before the VkDevice. A debug assertion checks the former.
//
// Heap usage
//   * Flush, Invalidate, SubmitTimeline and WaitTimeline never allocate from the heap.
//   * CreateBuffer / Buffer::Reset avoid heap allocation in the steady state (free lists are
//     pre-reserved), but a new memory block or an unusually fragmented block can still allocate.
//     They are intended for load time or level transitions, not for per-frame use. For per-frame
//     data, create one persistently mapped buffer up front and sub-divide it with a ring buffer.
//
// Status: not yet validated on real hardware. Please run with the Vulkan validation layers
// enabled and report any message they produce.
//
// Example
//   auto picked   = KytyVulkan::PickPhysicalDevice(instance);   // best GPU that meets the requirements
//   auto gpu      = picked->gpu;
//   auto families = picked->families;
//   auto support  = picked->support;                            // support.extensions -> VkDeviceCreateInfo
//   // ... create the VkDevice (enable timelineSemaphore if support.timelineSemaphore) ...
//   KytyVulkan::Allocator allocator(gpu, device, support.memoryBudget);
//   KytyVulkan::Buffer    buffer;
//   if (allocator.CreateBuffer(1 << 20, VK_BUFFER_USAGE_UNIFORM_BUFFER_BIT,
//                              KytyVulkan::MemoryUsage::BarUpload, buffer) == VK_SUCCESS) {
//       std::memcpy(buffer.Mapped(), src, 1 << 20);
//       (void)allocator.Flush(buffer);  // no-op on coherent memory
//   }

